    },
    {
      "amount": 5,
      "unit": "ml",
      "name": "Caching and Faster Access"
    }
  ],
  "method": [
    "Imagine you're at a park with a big map, but instead of showing all the parks in your city, it only shows some nearby ones.",
    "When someone tells you to go 'to the park', they're telling you which specific location they want to see on the map. Similarly, when you type a website address into your browser's address bar, that's asking for a particular webpage.",
    "The DNS (Domain Name System) is like a phonebook in the sky—it translates human-readable domain names (like 'www.example.com') into numerical IP addresses (the numbers the computer can actually understand).",
    "When you type www.example.com into your browser, it doesn't know how to find this website directly. It sends an invisible message to the DNS server asking for its location.",
    "The DNS server then responds with the exact coordinates needed for that webpage—just like if the map had a section marked 'example.com'. Your computer uses these coordinates to locate and show you your desired page."
  ],
  "garnish": "Imagine opening a new window in Google Chrome. That's caching at work! The browser remembers where it got the pages from before, so next time it opens one, it can quickly find it without needing to ask DNS again. It’s like having a friend who knows the way to your favorite place."
}

